intro:
Followed a link from <a href="http://www.stellated.com/blog">Ned Batchelder's blog</a> to <a href="http://thedailywtf.com/">The Daily WTF</a>, a collection of reader-submitted programming gaffes and mis-features.

<p>
Here's one I ran across a few months ago, from a class that represented a timestamp:

<pre class="code">
    public boolean after(Time24 time) {
        int thisValue = getHour() 
                      + getMinute() 
                      + getSeconds();

        int timeValue = time.getHour() 
                      + time.getMinute() 
                      + time.getSeconds();
        
        return ( timeValue &lt; thisValue );
    }
</pre>

<p>
So, is 2:20 before or after 4:15?

Okay, so last night I'm having fun playing with Struts Tiles.  I've
got all my JSPs converted over from this:

<pre class="code">
&lt;jsp:insert page="Header.jsp" /&gt;
&lt;!-- Content --&gt;
&lt;jsp:insert page="Footer.jsp" /&gt;
</pre>

<p> to this:

<pre class="code">
&lt;tiles:insert definition="pageLayout"&gt;
  &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="content"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Content --&gt;
  &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
&lt;/tiles:insert&gt;
</pre>

<p> This is excellent.  This is excellent because I have also included
a "sidebar" in my template, so that I can do this on the pages where I
want to:

<pre class="code">
&lt;tiles:insert definition="pageLayout"&gt;
  &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="content"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Content --&gt;
  &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
  &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="sidebar"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Sidebar --&gt;
  &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
&lt;/tiles:insert&gt;
</pre>

<p> Pretty snazzy.  Oh, and I've defined a nice little tile to handle
the creation of sections for the sidebar.  Checky-checky:

<pre class="code">
&lt;%@ taglib uri="/WEB-INF/struts-tiles.tld" prefix="tiles" %&gt;
&lt;div class="navitem"&gt;
  &lt;span class="navtitle"&gt;&lt;tiles:get name="title" /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;tiles:get name="content" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</pre>

<p> And the documentation says that you can nest Tiles arbitrarily,
which is a huge step over the old way of doing things.  So now I can
have a page that knows what content should be displayed, and what
content should go into the sidebar, but does <em>not</em> know how any
of the layout works.  Totally layout agnostic.  Very cool.

<p> Except, problems arise when I try to do this:

<pre class="code">
&lt;tiles:insert definition="pageLayout"&gt;
  &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="content"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Content --&gt;
  &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
  &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="sidebar"&gt;
    &lt;tiles:insert definition="navItem"&gt;
      &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="title"&gt;
        Sample Nav Title
      &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
      &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="content"&gt;
        &lt;ul class="navlist"&gt;
          &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;
          &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;
          &lt;li&gt;Item 3&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
      &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
    &lt;/tiles:insert&gt;
  &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
&lt;/tiles:insert&gt;
</pre>

<p> See what kind of incestuous inside-out thinking I'm trying to
perpetrate there?  Huh?  Do ya?  Yep: I'm trying to insert one tile
into the value of a <code>put</code> for another tile.

<p> My app server freaks out completely, saying that it's illegal to
call <code>flush()</code> inside a custom tag.  Bummer.  I'm going to
have to figure out a better way to do this.  But lemme tell ya, with
limitations like this, I'm ready to dig in and write my own damn
templating system.

body:
(By the way, I've attached all the source code for this, as well as Rick Hightower's Tiles Tutorial to this entry.  You can find them all in the sidebar under "Attachments.")

<p>Here's the deal: I want to put a rock inside a little box, and then
put that little box inside a big box.  So the first thing I do is make
a "BigBox" Tile:


<p> File 1: BigBox.jsp:

<pre class="code">
&lt;%@ taglib uri="/WEB-INF/struts-tiles.tld" prefix="tiles" %&gt;
&lt;div style="border: 3px double black"&gt;
  &lt;tiles:get name="content" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</pre>


<p> And I'm going to need a "LittleBox" tile:

<p> File 2: LittleBox.jsp:

<pre class="code">
&lt;%@ taglib uri="/WEB-INF/struts-tiles.tld" prefix="tiles" %&gt;
&lt;div style="border: 1px solid red; margin: 2px; padding: 2px;"&gt;
  &lt;tiles:get name="content" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</pre>

<p> And finally, I'm going to need a page that'll use the other two.
(I'm leaving out the XML config files.  Those are covered adequately
in <a href="http://www.arc-mind.com/papers/advanced-tiles.pdf">Rick
Hightower's excellent Tiles tutorial</a>.)

<p> File 3: BoxTest.jsp

<pre class="code">
&lt;%@ taglib uri="/WEB-INF/struts-tiles.tld" prefix="tiles" %&gt;
&lt;tiles:insert definition="bigBox"&gt;
    &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="content"&gt;
        &lt;tiles:insert definition="littleBox" <span style="color: red">flush="false"</span>&gt;
            &lt;tiles:put type="string" name="content"&gt;
                rock
            &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
        &lt;/tiles:insert&gt;
    &lt;/tiles:put&gt;
&lt;/tiles:insert&gt;
</pre>

<p> See that highlighted <code>flush="false"</code>?  That's what
makes the whole thing work.  It's not covered in Hightower's tutorial,
or in any of the Struts docs I could find, so I'm putting it here.  Now I can
do all the freaky stuff that I wanted to do from the beginning.

<p> And now, off to movie night.

intro:
I was just reading <a
href="http://martinfowler.com/bliki/ErraticTestFailure.html">this
excellent post on debugging</a> over at Martin Fowler's Bliki, where he says, "Early on in my
programming youth an older programmer taught me the first rule of
debugging - the bug's always in your code, not the compiler," and that
reminded me of a time when that wasn't true.

<p> It was way back in the stone ages of 2002, and I was a newbie
programmer working on some servlet code.  In this particular code, I
needed to compare the <code>password</code> parameter passed in via a
login form with the password stored in the database.  Like so:

<pre class="code">
try {
    String user = request.getParameter("user");
    String pass = request.getParameter("pass");

    if (!pass.equals(getPassForUser(user))) {
        throw new InvalidLoginException();
    }
}
catch (InvalidLoginException rethrown) {
    throw rethrown;
}
catch (Exception e) {
    throw new SecurityException(e);
}
</pre>

<p> The trouble was: it was letting everyone log in, without actually
checking the password.  There were several layers of bugs.

<p> <b>Layer 1</b>

<p> First, that <code>getPassForUser</code> method was coded by my
boss, and looked like this:

<pre class="code">
public String getPassForUser(String user)
{
    String p = null;
    try {
        // Do some database access stuff and
        p = rs.getString("password");
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {}
    return p;
}
</pre>

<p> See the problem?  In the event that there's anything wrong, this
method simply returns null.  I have <a href="archive/315">commented on this practice</a> before.

<p><b>Layer 2</b>

<p> The graphic designer had been working on the login page, and had
used Macromedia Dreamweaver to do the editing.  DW has a pernicious
little "feature" that silently overwrites the names of form fields.
If you copy and past a field named "foo", the pasted field will be
named "foo2".  The net result was that the fields in the login form
got renamed to "user2" and "pass2".  So both the <code>user</code> and <code>pass</code> parameter values
are now null.

<p><b>Layer 3</b>

<p> The crux of the problem is the line that actually compares the
password from the database to the one supplied by the user:


<pre class="code">
    if (!pass.equals(getPassForUser(user))) {
        throw new InvalidLoginException();
    }
</pre>

<p> When I wrote the code, I knew that if <code>pass</code> were null
this line would throw a NullPointerException, and if
<code>getPassForUser</code> returned null, then it would throw an
InvalidLoginException.  Either way, an Exception is thrown, and the
user can't log in.

<p> Except there was a bug in the Java Runtime for that version of the
Netscape Application Server.  I noticed the problems with the HTML
form pretty quickly.  But the Servlet was still letting people log in
as long as they didn't supply a username or password.  This had to be
fixed.

<p> After a bit of fiddling, I ran the following test code:

<pre class="code">
System.out.println(null.equals(null));
</pre>

<p> Sure enough, under Netscape's application server, the statement
simply printed "true" to the main log file, in complete defiance of
the Java language specification, which states that calling a method on
a null object reference should throw a NullPointerException.

<p> So anyway, the moral to the story, kids, is that once in a
<em>very</em> great while the bug <em>is</em> in the compiler. (Well,
in this case in the JRE.)

Here's one for the J2EE crowd.  I'm working on a few JSPs today, and I keep seeing this:

<pre class="code">
&lt;% } } } %&gt;
</pre>

<p> Leaving aside the extensive use of scriptlets in these JSPs... yes, that's what it looks like: the end of three different code blocks.  It's like writing this in normal code:

<pre class="code">
if (...) {
    while (...) {
     if (...) {
   } else {
       if (...) {
            }}}
</pre>

<p> Nobody wants to read that garbage.  And the real kicker is that the first <code>if</code> <em>still</em> isn't closed.  So, for all you consultants out there, here's the rule: be consistent and logical with your indentation style, or I'll have you killed.

