Just for the sake of discussion, I'll show you the code the way I re-wrote it:

<pre class="code">
int colHeight = Math.round(fooArray.length/4.0f);
int row = 0;
int col = 0;
</pre>

<p> The rest of the code in this particular JSP was similarly...refactorable.  About 400 lines of garbage collapsed down to about 120.  It's still not perfect, but it's a hell of a lot better.

<p> And I'm fully aware of the trade-offs associated with re-writing code, or doing "code cleanup."  This particular page has already eaten several hours of developer time for trivial changes.  Reworking it may not make any money for my employer, but leaving it the way it was certainly would have wasted more than the few dollars I got paid to fix it.

intro:
When I run a development team, the following will get you either shot
or fired, depending on whether I have a pistol to hand.

<pre class="code">
int mid = Math.round(fooArray.length/4.0f);
int k = 0;
int j = 0;
int n = 0;
int ii = 0;
int kk = 0;
int mm = 0;
int nn = 0;
</pre>

<p> You have been warned.

I've ranted before about returning null.  Just when I thought I'd seen
it all, I find this:

<pre class="code">
class Abomination {
    public boolean ignore;

    public Foo getFoo() throws FooException {
        try {
            return doDataRetrievalStuff();
        }
        catch (Exception e) {
            if (ignore) return null;
            else throw new FooException(e);
        }
    }
}
</pre>


<p> Listen up, you primitive screwheads. <em>Never</em> ignore
exceptions.  Furthermore, you just multiplied the testing burden for
this code.  I have to test it for cases <code>ignore=true</code>
<em>and</em> <code>ignore=false</code>.  I have to make sure my code
handles both the possible FooException <em>and</em> checks for
<code>null</code>, as well as simply doing the right thing with the
Foo when all goes well.

<p> So, to the programmer who wrote this: when I find you, I'm going
to grab you by the ear and walk you over to your boss, and explain to
him/her what a muppet you are.

<p> And then I'm going to explain to him/her that automated testing
and QA does <em>not</em> obviate code reviews, <em>especially</em>
when you're building a framework that other developers will build on.

<p> <i>Gah!</i>

Here's today's winner:

<pre class="code">
protected Vector resultBeans = new Vector();

public void doFoo() {
    try {
        // Do some database stuff
        if (noResults) {
            badData = true;
            return;
        }
    }
    catch (Exception e) {
        badInput = true;
        return;
    }

    if (badData || badInput) return;

    // Populate the resultBeans Vector.
}
</pre>    

<p> You can't make this stuff up.

<p> Sure there's the totally obvious stupidity.  But what puts this on
a new level is that <em>this method will never show any sign of
failure</em>.  I can't check for a <code>null</code> return value, and
this method will never throw any Exception.

<p> "Primitive screwheads" just doesn't quite say it.

Evidently, like spiders or snakes or other creepy-crawly things, the
keyword <code>this</code> scares some programmers.  I don't think it's
a stretch to think that these programmers also fear daylight, new
foods, and girls.

<p> I'm seeing a lot of code that avoids using <code>this</code>.  On
the one hand, that means more girls for me.  On the other, it means I
have to put up with some kid's clever avoidance of <code>this</code>:

<pre class="code">
public setFoo(Hoofa aFoo) {
    foo = aFoo;
}
</pre>

<p> I see this a lot too:

<pre class="code">
public setFoo(Hoofa foo) {
    mFoo = foo;
}
</pre>

<p> Let's stick to the conventions, kids.

I keep running across code that is very nearly <a
href="http://thedailywtf.com">WTF</a>-worthy, but <a
href="http://thedailywtf.com/forums/38234/ShowPost.aspx">today's
WTF</a> surpasses anything I've seen lately.  Reprinted here for
posterity:

<pre class="code">
public Object findRecord(String table, int i) {
  Object r = null;
  r = executeQuery("select * from " + table + " where id = " + i);
  if (r == null) r = new ClassNotFoundException();
  return r;
}
</pre>

<p> It's got a kind of poetry to it, really.
